---
/**
 * FaqGrouped — every entry in the `faq` collection, under the four
 * category headings, for /faq and only /faq.
 *
 * WHY THIS IS A SECOND COMPONENT AND NOT A PROP ON Faq.astro. Core-pages
 * spec §3.1 settles it: "/faq renders all 14 under four headings … That is
 * a different layout, not a parameter." Faq.astro renders one flat,
 * filtered, limited list with a section header and a "see all" link, sized
 * to sit between other sections on a page about something else. This is
 * the page's entire content: no limit, no surface filter, four headings, a
 * two-column group shape at width, and a question heading level so that a
 * screen-reader user can move through fourteen questions by heading rather
 * than by tabbing every disclosure.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE <details> ITEM MARKUP AND ITS CSS ARE DUPLICATED FROM Faq.astro.
 * DELIBERATELY. READ THIS BEFORE "FIXING" IT.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * Extracting the item into one shared component was tried first, because
 * it is obviously the right instinct, and it was measured rather than
 * argued about. The bar Task 9 was set: / and /pricing — two LIVE pages
 * that render Faq.astro — must come out byte-identical from a full-file
 * diff of dist/ taken before and after. They do not, and cannot.
 *
 * Astro scopes component styles by stamping a per-component attribute on
 * the elements the component's own template writes. Moving the item into
 * src/components/FaqItem.astro moves its CSS there too (Faq.astro's
 * `.faq-question` / `.faq-icon` rules stop reaching a child component's
 * inner elements — only a child's ROOT element inherits the parent's
 * scope, so the <summary> and both <span>s would lose their styling
 * entirely). The child then earns its own scope attribute, and every
 * <details>, <summary>, <span> and answer <div> on the homepage gains a
 * second one. Measured, on the probe:
 *
 *     before  .faq-item[data-astro-cid-oaufy27u]{border-bottom:...}
 *     after   .faq-item[data-astro-cid-6rmelcr5]{border-bottom:...}
 *
 *     dist/index.html          61,434 → 61,452 bytes, first diff at 9,678
 *     dist/pricing/index.html  46,558 → 46,567 bytes, first diff at 5,525
 *
 * There is no arrangement of the extraction that avoids this: the only way
 * to keep the emitted attributes identical is to keep the CSS in the file
 * that emits the markup, which is the thing extraction undoes. A `:global`
 * escape hatch removes the scope attribute from the selector instead,
 * which changes the same bytes in the other direction.
 *
 * So the ruling's fallback applies, and it is the right one: a subtle
 * rendering difference shipped to two live pages, to avoid a duplicate, is
 * a worse outcome than the duplicate. The probe was reverted and dist/
 * confirmed byte-identical to the baseline again before this file was
 * written.
 *
 * WHAT THAT COSTS AND HOW IT IS PAID. Two copies of a disclosure widget
 * can drift. They are kept honest by tests/faq-grouped.test.ts, which
 * asserts that the item's visual contract — the border hairlines, the
 * 1.125rem display-face question, the suppressed native marker, the
 * plus/minus icon geometry and its [open] flip, the focus ring, the 60ch
 * answer measure — is declared identically in both files. That test is the
 * reason this duplicate is maintainable rather than merely tolerated. If
 * Astro ever gains a way to share scoped CSS without changing emitted
 * markup, delete the duplicate and the test together.
 *
 * WHAT IS NOT DUPLICATED: the section ground, the container measure, the
 * group shape, the heading levels and the JSON-LD are all this file's own.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 * ONE FAQPage BLOCK, FOR ALL FOURTEEN.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * Spec §4 gives this page the long-tail intent and says it is carried "via
 * FAQPage structured data across all 14". So: one block, not one per
 * group, and /faq renders no <Faq> at all — Faq.astro emits its own block
 * whenever `emitSchema` is left at its default, and two FAQPage blocks on
 * one page is a structured-data defect rather than twice the signal.
 * Asserted in tests/faq-grouped.test.ts against the built page.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 * HEADINGS.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * The page's <h1> is in faq.astro. Each group title is an <h2>. Each
 * question is an <h3> INSIDE its <summary> — <summary>'s content model is
 * "phrasing content, optionally intermixed with heading content", so this
 * is valid, and it is what gives a screen-reader user a heading list of
 * all fourteen questions instead of fourteen unlabelled disclosures in a
 * flat run. Faq.astro uses a bare <span> because six questions inside a
 * page about something else do not warrant fourteen headings' worth of
 * outline; here they are the document.
 *
 * Native <details>/<summary> throughout, for the reasons in Faq.astro's
 * header: zero JavaScript, keyboard-operable for free, announced correctly
 * without any ARIA bookkeeping.
 */
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content'
import Container from '../components/Container.astro'
import { faqPageSchema, markdownToPlainText } from '../lib/structured-data'

/**
 * The four groups, in the order spec §3.2 lists them: "Cost & Pricing /
 * Helpers & Sources / Process & Timing / Replacement". `category` is the
 * required enum on the faq collection (src/content/config.ts), added for
 * exactly this layout.
 *
 * The titles are the spec's own words. They are NOT derived from the
 * category values — "cost" is a field value and "Cost & Pricing" is a
 * heading a visitor reads, and the two should be free to differ.
 */
const GROUPS = [
  { category: 'cost', title: 'Cost & Pricing' },
  { category: 'sources', title: 'Helpers & Sources' },
  { category: 'process', title: 'Process & Timing' },
  { category: 'replacement', title: 'Replacement' },
] as const

/*
 * Sorted ONCE, globally, then partitioned. Sorting the whole collection
 * before splitting it means each group comes out in `order` sequence
 * without a second sort, and it is the same comparator Faq.astro uses, so
 * a question cannot appear in one sequence on the homepage and another
 * here.
 */
const allFaqs = (await getCollection('faq')).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order)

const groups = await Promise.all(
  GROUPS.map(async (group) => ({
    ...group,
    entries: await Promise.all(
      allFaqs
        .filter((entry) => entry.data.category === group.category)
        .map(async (entry) => ({ entry, Content: (await entry.render()).Content })),
    ),
  })),
)

/*
 * THE BUILD FAILS RATHER THAN DROPPING AN ENTRY.
 *
 * This page's whole claim is that it is the complete list. If a fifth
 * category is ever added to the schema and not to GROUPS above, every
 * entry carrying it would silently vanish from the one page that exists to
 * show all of them — and nothing else would notice: the page would still
 * have four headings, still have questions under each, still emit valid
 * FAQPage data, and still pass every structural guard in the suite. The
 * only visible symptom would be a smaller number of questions than
 * DirectHired has answers for.
 *
 * A count comparison is enough to catch it because the filter is on a
 * required enum: an entry is in exactly one group or in none.
 */
const placed = groups.reduce((total, group) => total + group.entries.length, 0)
if (placed !== allFaqs.length) {
  const missing = allFaqs
    .filter((entry) => !GROUPS.some((group) => group.category === entry.data.category))
    .map((entry) => `${entry.id} (category: ${entry.data.category})`)
  throw new Error(
    `FaqGrouped: ${allFaqs.length - placed} of ${allFaqs.length} faq entries have no group. ` +
      `Add the category to GROUPS in this file: ${missing.join(', ')}`,
  )
}

/*
 * One block, every entry, in the same order the page renders them.
 * markdownToPlainText converts each raw body for acceptedAnswer.text
 * exactly as Faq.astro does — it does not touch the visible <Content />
 * rendering below.
 */
const faqSchema = faqPageSchema(
  allFaqs.map((entry) => ({
    question: entry.data.question,
    answer: markdownToPlainText(entry.body ?? ''),
  })),
)
---

<section class="faq-grouped">
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <Container>
    {
      groups.map((group) => (
        <div class="faq-group">
          <h2 class="group-title">{group.title}</h2>

          <div class="group-items">
            {group.entries.map(({ entry, Content }) => (
              <details class="faq-item">
                <summary class="faq-question">
                  <h3 class="faq-question-text">{entry.data.question}</h3>
                  <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true" />
                </summary>
                <div class="faq-answer">
                  <Content />
                </div>
              </details>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </Container>
</section>

<style>
  .faq-grouped {
    padding-block: var(--space-section);
    /* White, between the cream hero above and FinalCta's cream below.
       FinalCta is fixed at --color-surface because the homepage renders
       the same component, which fixes this one. Registered in
       tests/section-rhythm.test.ts; grounds are judged as a sequence, so
       do not flip this one alone. */
    background: var(--color-surface-raised);
  }

  /*
   * A group is a titled band with a hairline over it — the shape /about's
   * record rows and /find-your-helper's timings use, at group scale. The
   * rule is what makes four groups read as four groups when every one of
   * them is a stack of identical closed rows.
   *
   * Decorative --color-border (~1.3:1, see tokens.css), never
   * --color-brand-teal: teal as a drawn line is reserved for block 07's
   * crossbar and FinalCta's mark, and spending it on section furniture is
   * what stops those two meaning anything.
   */
  .faq-group {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-6);
    padding-top: var(--space-8);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .faq-group + .faq-group {
    margin-top: var(--space-16);
  }

  /*
   * --size-h3, not --size-h2. These head a sub-part of a page the <h1> has
   * already opened, and the same reasoning applies here as on /about,
   * /pricing and /why-directhired: setting them at section-header scale
   * would put two competing display sizes on one screen.
   */
  .group-title {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--size-h3);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------------------------
   * THE ITEM. Duplicated from src/sections/Faq.astro — see this file's
   * header for the measurement that ruled extraction out, and
   * tests/faq-grouped.test.ts for what keeps the two copies honest.
   * Every declaration below is byte-for-byte what Faq.astro declares,
   * except `.faq-question-text`, which exists only here because the
   * question is an <h3> on this page and a <span> there.
   * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

  .faq-item {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .faq-item:first-child {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .faq-question {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    padding-block: var(--space-6);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
    color: var(--color-ink);
    cursor: pointer;
    list-style: none;
  }

  /* Remove the native disclosure marker so the custom indicator is the only one. */
  .faq-question::-webkit-details-marker {
    display: none;
  }

  .faq-question::marker {
    content: '';
  }

  .faq-question:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--color-accent);
    outline-offset: 4px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
  }

  /*
   * The <h3> carries none of its own type — it inherits the summary's, so
   * the question is set exactly as Faq.astro sets it and the heading is a
   * semantic addition rather than a visual one. Without the resets it
   * would render at --size-h3 (24px) against the flat list's 18px.
   *
   * `min-width: 0` is load-bearing at 320px: a flex item's default
   * min-width is auto, which refuses to shrink below its longest
   * unbreakable run and pushes the icon off the container instead of
   * wrapping the question.
   */
  .faq-question-text {
    min-width: 0;
    margin: 0;
    font-family: inherit;
    font-size: inherit;
    font-weight: inherit;
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: inherit;
    /* Left-aligned ragged right, not the global heading `balance`: a
       balanced two-line question centres its own line breaks against an
       icon pinned to the right edge, which reads as a mis-set line. */
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .faq-icon {
    position: relative;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    width: 1.25rem;
    height: 1.25rem;
  }

  .faq-icon::before,
  .faq-icon::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    top: 50%;
    left: 50%;
    background: var(--color-accent);
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
    transition: transform var(--duration-base) var(--ease);
  }

  .faq-icon::before {
    width: 12px;
    height: 2px;
  }

  .faq-icon::after {
    width: 2px;
    height: 12px;
  }

  .faq-item[open] .faq-icon::after {
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%) scaleY(0);
  }

  .faq-answer {
    max-width: 60ch;
    padding-bottom: var(--space-6);
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  .faq-answer :global(p:first-child) {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .faq-answer :global(p:last-child) {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  /*
   * THE BLOCK SPACING, and as of 2026-08-16 it is part of the duplicated
   * item rather than this file's own.
   *
   * It arrived here alone because Task 9's byte-identical ruling for / and
   * /pricing forbade touching Faq.astro, so the two surfaces differed in one
   * respect on purpose and the homepage went on running its multi-paragraph
   * answers together. That ruling was narrowed by the person who made it —
   * it existed to stop an extraction refactor altering live pages, not to
   * freeze a readability defect — and Faq.astro now declares these two rules
   * identically. See its header for what changed on the two live pages.
   *
   * The list RESET is still untouched on every surface. global.css's `ul, ol
   * { list-style: none; padding: 0 }` means a bulleted answer renders as
   * unmarked stacked lines here and on the homepage alike; that is a
   * decision about the site's list treatment, not about this widget.
   */
  .faq-answer :global(p),
  .faq-answer :global(ul),
  .faq-answer :global(ol) {
    margin-block: var(--space-4) 0;
  }

  .faq-answer :global(li + li) {
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------------------------
   * End of the duplicated item. Everything below is this file's own.
   * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

  /* --color-accent (5.83:1 on white), never --color-brand-teal, which
     measures 2.89:1 and carries no text anywhere on this site. */
  .faq-answer :global(a) {
    color: var(--color-accent);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-accent);
  }

  .faq-answer :global(a:hover) {
    color: var(--color-accent-hover);
    border-color: var(--color-accent-hover);
  }

  /*
   * 64em = --bp-wide (tokens.css), the site's established content-layout
   * switch — Hero, Process and TwoSidedMatch all use it, and that token's
   * entry lists its consumers. Add this file there if the list is ever
   * regenerated. The literal is repeated by hand because custom properties
   * do not resolve inside a media condition.
   *
   * WHY THE GROUP TITLE MOVES INTO A COLUMN RATHER THAN STAYING ABOVE.
   * Faq.astro caps its column at the 48rem reading measure because it is a
   * block inside a longer page. This page IS the column, and at 1280 a
   * single 48rem stack leaves the right 45% of the container empty for the
   * whole scroll — the defect /about's Singapore block was measured and
   * fixed for. Putting the four titles in a fixed 14rem gutter uses that
   * width for the page's own structure: the title stays beside its
   * questions while they are read, and the questions still land on a
   * single left axis.
   *
   * 14rem holds "Helpers & Sources" — the longest title — on two lines at
   * --size-h3. minmax(0, 1fr) on the second track lets it shrink below its
   * content's min-content width instead of pushing the row wider than the
   * container, which is what would put a horizontal scrollbar on the page.
   */
  @media (min-width: 64em) {
    .faq-group {
      grid-template-columns: 14rem minmax(0, 1fr);
      column-gap: var(--space-12);
      align-items: start;
    }
  }
</style>
